  "content": {
    "title": "Houston Texans sign Will Anderson to 3-year, $150 million extension",
    "text": "Houston Texans defensive end Will Anderson Jr. (51) reacts after sacking Kansas City Chiefs quarterback Patrick Mahomes during the first half of an NFL football game in Kansas City, Mo., Sunday, Dec. 7, 2025.Brett Coomer/Houston ChronicleThe Houston Texans have given Will Anderson Jr. a three-year contract extension worth $150 million, two sources with direct knowledge of the deal told the Houston Chronicle. Of that $150 million, $134 million is guaranteed, both sources said. Anderson’s deal keeps him in Houston through the 2030 season. He already had two years left on his rookie contract, including the $21.5 million fifth-year option that Houston exercised for the 2027 season.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.ONLY 25¢His new money will kick in and count against the team’s salary cap in 2028. Article continues below this adIn addition to the money, his agent, Nicole Lynn, negotiated a no-trade clause. The new contract makes him the highest-paid defensive player in NFL history in terms of average per year, surpassing Micah Parson, who set the record last offseason with a four-year, $188 million deal ($46.5 million per). He is one of the few known defensive players in the league with a no-trade clause, allowing him to refuse a trade if the Texans were to attempt a deal, but that is unlikely given how much he means to this team. Anderson, 24, entering his fourth season with the Texans, was drafted No. 3 overall in 2023. He’s been to two Pro Bowls and was an All-Pro last season. A three-time team captain, Anderson has 30 sacks in his first three seasons, including 12 in 2025. In addition to those 12 sacks, he had 20 tackles for loss, three forced fumbles, two fumble recoveries and one defensive touchdown. Article continues below this adHe was also a finalist for the NFL’s defensive player of the year. “I love Will Anderson,” coach DeMeco Ryans said last month at the NFL’s league meetings. “I love everything he brings to our organization. A guy who does everything the right way. Everything you want, everything I've asked of him.”He thought back to Anderson’s top-30 visit in 2023, a little more than a month before they drafted him. Anderson was the only player they brought in that day. “That meeting is still the best 30 visit I’ve ever seen,” Ryans said. “He’s that type of guy. He’s a leader in all respects.”General manager Nick Caserio has a history of paying his top players and Anderson is just the latest to secure a long-term deal. Last offseason, he made cornerback Derek Stingley Jr. the highest-paid cornerback in the NFL, while also making Jalen Pitre the highest-paid nickel back. Soon, quarterback C.J. Stroud is expected to receive a rich contract extension, barring any unforeseen circumstances or a significant dip in his production. But Stroud could see an extension next offseason as Houston may try to stagger the two. He too, will likely become one of the highest-paid players in the NFL. Anderson has previously said he wanted to remain in Houston for his entire career.His deal, at least, keeps one of their franchise cornerstones in Houston for the foreseeable future. April 17, 2026|Updated April 17, 2026 1:58 p.m.Jonathan M. Alexander is a staff writer for the Houston Chronicle covering the Houston Texans and NFL. Alexander is a Charlotte native and graduated in 2013 from N.C. Central University, an HBCU in Durham, N.C. He’s covered a variety of beats in his career from K-12 schools, public safety, town government, recruiting, Duke basketball, UNC basketball and football at the Raleigh New & Observer, and most recently spent two years covering the Carolina Panthers for the Charlotte Observer. He has twice earned APSE National Top 10 honors for his writing and reporting. He broke one of the biggest stories on the Panthers beat in November 2021, when he revealed that the Panthers were planning to meet with quarterback Cam Newton with the intentions to sign him one year after releasing him. In his free time, Jonathan enjoys coaching youth basketball, trying new restaurants and hanging with friends. He has one sister, Tyler, who has lived in Houston for six years. His parents, Stanley and Becky, who are his biggest influences, are both retired social workers.",
